Ryoku's alpha series (through v0.1.1-alpha) began as a fork of Omarchy, created by
David Heinemeier Hansson and contributors (https://github.com/basecamp/omarchy).
The beta tree was pruned and rebuilt from an empty root; its installer, shell,
theming, boot art, tooling and system definition are Ryoku's own.

The Ryoku shell is an original, custom shell. Its frame-blob rendering (the
Ryoku.Blobs QML/C++ plugin) and parts of its animation and motion curves are
adapted from the Caelestia shell:

  https://github.com/caelestia-dots/shell

Caelestia is released under the GNU General Public License version 3; the adapted
portions remain under that license, compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3.

Portions of the Ryoku display configuration UI are adapted from
DankMaterialShell, created by Avenge Media LLC and contributors:

  https://github.com/AvengeMedia/DankMaterialShell

DankMaterialShell is released under the MIT License. Copyright (c) 2025
Avenge Media LLC.

The configurable frame-bar layout, bounded rail/widget catalogue organisation,
and Bar Studio workflow are adapted from OkShell by John Oberhauser:

 https://github.com/JohnOberhauser/OkShell/tree/87a9d09d923163cd03f396a395be3bb02b335b6e

OkShell is released under the GNU General Public License version 3. The adapted
frame-bar areas remain GPL-3.0-compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.

The Stage Editor (ryoku/shell/quickshell/stage/) is based on the Edit Mode of
ii-p3drovfx by P3DROVFX and contributors
(https://github.com/P3DROVFX/ii-p3drovfx), which builds on ii-vynx by
vaguesyntax (https://github.com/vaguesyntax/ii-vynx) and end-4's
illogical-impulse (https://github.com/end-4/dots-hyprland). It is a modified
version: Ryoku runs it over its own desktop, widgets, wallpaper, visualizer and
depth services, gives it the Ryoku look, and leaves out its lock screen, bar and
dock editing. ii-p3drovfx is released under the GNU General Public License
version 3; the ported portions keep that license, compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3.

The Stage Editor's symbolic icons (ryoku/shell/quickshell/stage/assets/icons/)
come from end-4's illogical-impulse (https://github.com/end-4/dots-hyprland) by
way of iNiR by snowarch (https://github.com/snowarch/inir). They are released
under the GNU General Public License version 3 and keep that license and their
authors' credit; PROVENANCE.txt in that folder lists them.

The ii dock design (ryoku/shell/quickshell/shell/modules/dock/ii/) is based on
the dock of the same shell, ii-p3drovfx by P3DROVFX, Scrimas and contributors
(https://github.com/P3DROVFX/ii-p3drovfx), itself descended from end-4's
illogical-impulse (https://github.com/end-4/dots-hyprland). It is a modified
version: Ryoku reads and writes its options through its own shared dock store
and stage configuration, takes its global state, theme, window data and
keybinds from the Ryoku shell and window-manager seams, and adds the store
bridge that keeps the design in sync with the other dock designs. It is
released under the GNU General Public License version 3; the ported portions
keep that license, compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3.

Kit's Dev tools, Commands, Typing test and Phone tabs
(ryoku/shell/quickshell/keys/) and the phone module behind the Phone tab
(ryoku/shell/quickshell/shell/modules/phone/) are based on the cheatsheet pages,
phone services and Phone sidebar of ii-p3drovfx by P3DROVFX and contributors
(https://github.com/P3DROVFX/ii-p3drovfx). They are modified versions: Ryoku
draws them in its own look inside Kit and the Hub, keeps only the developer
tools its users reach for, generates random values from /dev/urandom instead of
Math.random, runs the phone services in the shell and lets Kit drive them over
IPC, reads Android manifests with its own parser instead of pyaxmlparser, uses
scrcpy for the phone webcam and microphone instead of DroidCam, installs the
phone packages on demand, and routes window placement through the
window-manager seam. They are released under the GNU General Public License
version 3; the ported portions keep that license, compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3.

The word lists and key sounds under ryoku/shell/quickshell/keys/typing/assets/
come from Monkeytype (https://github.com/monkeytypegame/monkeytype), released
under the GNU General Public License version 3 only; the pinned upstream commit
is recorded in the manifests beside them.

The built-in desktop sounds under ryoku/shell/ipc/assets/ come from the
freedesktop.org sound theme, renamed after the event they play for. Per that
theme's credits: the notification sound (message-new-instant) is by Damien
Sandras, GNU General Public License version 2 or later; battery-full (complete)
is by Dr. Richard Boulanger et al, Creative Commons Attribution 3.0 Unported;
the shutter (camera-shutter) is by freesound user horsthorstensen and
volume-change (audio-volume-change) by Lucas McCallister, both Creative Commons
Attribution-ShareAlike; the warning, plug, unplug and device sounds
(dialog-warning, power-plug, power-unplug, device-added, device-removed) are by
Ivica Bukvic, Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike.

The Redux dictation adapter (ryoku/hub/backend/assets/redux/adapter.py) is
voxtype-redux-adapter v0.2.0 by Sipper1236
(https://github.com/Sipper1236/voxtype-redux-adapter), shipped unmodified
inside ryoku-hub and installed with the Redux model from the Hub's Dictation
page. It is released under the MIT License, which ships beside it and in the
notice the installer writes; that license is compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3
distribution. The installer builds parakeet.cpp by the parakeet.cpp authors
(https://github.com/mudler/parakeet.cpp, MIT) with its ggml submodule on the
user's machine and downloads Moondream's Parakeet Redux weights, derived from
NVIDIA Parakeet TDT 0.6B v3 (CC-BY-4.0); neither ships in a Ryoku package.

The default Vesktop and Equibop theme
(ryoku/apps/vesktop/themes/ryoku-discord.theme.css) is ryoku.theme.css v3.0.0
from ryoku-discord by Ron (niqqudim, https://github.com/niqqudim/ryoku-discord)
at commit d9a0f2d, shipped unmodified under a Ryoku file name so it never
replaces a copy a user installed by hand. It builds on refact0r's midnight and
system24 Discord themes
(https://github.com/refact0r/midnight-discord and
https://github.com/refact0r/system24, both MIT). The client loads the rest of
its styles, fonts and artwork from the upstream site when the theme is enabled;
none of that ships in a Ryoku package. It is released under the MIT License,
which ships unchanged beside it and is installed as
/usr/share/licenses/ryoku-desktop/LICENSE.ryoku-discord; that license is
compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.

The Lithic Gio icons (docs/lithic/library/go/symbols/) are Material Symbols by
Google (https://github.com/google/material-design-icons), the Sharp style at
weight 400, taken from the official SVGs at commit 10ecc147. They are modified
versions: the path data is converted to IconVG and the shapes are otherwise
unchanged. They are released under the Apache License 2.0, which ships
unchanged beside them with a NOTICE; that license is compatible with Ryoku's
GPL-3 distribution.
